[0m[0m
Two bugs were found and fixed in the C-to-Rust translation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 1: Incorrect [38;5;10mdatalen[0m parameter to [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m (files: [38;5;10mhash.rs[0m, [38;5;10mmain.rs[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The BLAKE-512 [38;5;10mupdate[0m function takes [38;5;10mdatalen[0m in [1mbits[22m, not bytes. The C code has two calling conventions:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mblake512()[0m top-level function**: Converts bytes→bits internally ([38;5;10minlen * 8[0m). The Rust translation of this was correct.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mhash_blake.c[0m and [38;5;10mPQCgenKAT_sign.c[0m**: Call [38;5;10mblakeX_update()[0m directly, passing [1mbyte counts[22m as the datalen (e.g., [38;5;10mblakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N)[0m where [38;5;10mSPX_N=24[0m). This means only 24 bits (3 bytes) are actually hashed per call — this is the intended behavior of the reference C code.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translator incorrectly assumed these callers needed byte→bit conversion and added [38;5;10m* 8[0m multiplications everywhere (e.g., [38;5;10m(SPX_N as u64) * 8[0m). This caused 8× more data to be hashed per call, producing completely wrong cryptographic outputs.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix[22m: Removed all [38;5;10m* 8[0m multiplications in [38;5;10mhash.rs[0m ([38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m, [38;5;10mhash_message[0m) and [38;5;10mmain.rs[0m ([38;5;10mkat_tr_init[0m, [38;5;10mkat_tr_absorb_label[0m, [38;5;10mkat_tr_absorb_u64[0m, [38;5;10mkat_tr_absorb_bytes[0m) to match the C calling convention exactly.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 2: AES-256-ECB buffer undersize (file: [38;5;10mrng.rs[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The [38;5;10maes256_ecb[0m function passed the 16-byte output buffer directly to OpenSSL's [38;5;10mCrypter::update()[0m, but the Rust [38;5;10mopenssl[0m crate requires the output buffer to be at least [38;5;10minput_size + block_size[0m (32 bytes) even with padding disabled.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix[22m: Used a 32-byte temporary buffer for the [38;5;10mCrypter::update()[0m call, then copied the first 16 bytes to the actual output.[0m[0m
